  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij}\n$$\n\nand, hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $R.$  Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1(-1\\cdot A) & =-1(-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-1([\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot 1 & (-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-1) & (-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\ne -A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
